  "final_text": "All four steps are done.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Logs' (index `kibana_sample_data_logs`) has 40 fields. The time field is `timestamp`. `response` is a text field with a `response.keyword` sub-field, which I used for the response-code grouping and the alert filter.\n\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-1bdf2328 Web logs' has exactly two panels:\n   - \"Requests over time\": a line chart of request count by `timestamp` date histogram.\n   - \"Requests by response code\": a bar chart of request count by `response.keyword` terms (top 20).\n\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-1bdf2328 5xx errors' is a `.es-query` rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`, checked every 1m. It counts documents where `response.keyword` is between \"500\" and \"599\" over a 5-minute window and fires when the count is greater than 10. The rule was created disabled, so I enabled it.\n\n4. **Edit and read-back:** The response-code panel (index 1) is now titled \"Responses by code\". The rule read back as `enabled: true`, status `ok`.\n\nChoices I made:\n- The 500\u2013599 range is a lexical range on `response.keyword`. That is correct for three-digit codes, but it would also match a non-numeric value that sorts between \"500\" and \"599\". I did not verify the field's values against the index.\n- The dashboard's group-by uses the `.keyword` sub-field rather than the text field, because text fields can't be aggregated.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
